  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+1,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, we take the graph $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}.$ After this transformation, the point $(a,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(a+1,b)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(a+1,b+2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(a,b)$ lies on the graph of $y={x}^{2}$, we have that ${a}^{2}=b.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$ Substituting $x=a+1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(a+1-1{)}^{2}+2 \\\\  & ={a}^{2}+2 \\\\  & =b+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(a+1,b+2)$ must lie on $y=(x-1{)}^{2}+2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
